In a **square matrix** (rows = columns = n) the **left diagonal** joins the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner. Its elements have **i == j**, so one loop is enough.

```java
int[][] a = {
    {5, 2, 8, 1},
    {9, 4, 3, 7},
    {6, 5, 2, 3},
    {4, 8, 1, 6}
};
int ls = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
    ls = ls + a[i][i];
System.out.println(ls);
```

```java
int[][] a = {
    {3, 8, 1},
    {6, 4, 9},
    {2, 7, 5}
};
int ls = 0, rs = 0, once = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (i == j)
            ls = ls + a[i][j];
        if (i + j == 2)
            rs = rs + a[i][j];
        if (i == j || i + j == 2)
            once = once + a[i][j];
    }
System.out.println(ls);
System.out.println(rs);
System.out.println(once);
```

The test **i == j || i + j == 2** adds an element once even if it is on both diagonals.

sections.body_md:
An **initialiser list** gives the values at the time of declaration, one inner list for each row, and no sizes are written.

```java
int[][] fare = {
    {12, 18, 25},
    {10, 15, 20}
};
System.out.println(fare.length + " " + fare[0].length + " " + fare[1][2]);
```

Output: `2 3 20` (rows, columns and the last element).

**Default values** of a new array: int `0`, double `0.0`, boolean `false`, String `null`.

**Valid subscripts** are 0 to length - 1. Otherwise the program stops with an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException, for example `java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 3 out of bounds for length 3` for `a[3][0]` on a table with 3 rows. A whole row cannot be given a single value: `a[1] = 7;` gives "incompatible types: int cannot be converted to int[]".

To **accept** a table, read the order, create the array, then use **nested loops**: the outer loop over the rows and the inner loop over the columns, reading `a[i][j] = sc.nextInt();`. The elements are read in **row-major order**.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class GridReader {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int r = sc.nextInt();
        int c = sc.nextInt();
        int[][] a = new int[r][c];
        for (int i = 0; i < r; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < c; j++)
                a[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
        for (int i = 0; i < r; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < c; j++)
                System.out.print(a[i][j] + " ");
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

For the input 2 4 followed by the rows 31 8 15 22 and 6 40 17 9 the output is:

```
31 8 15 22
6 40 17 9
```

To **display** in matrix format print each element without a line break and put an empty `System.out.println();` after the inner loop. Without it the output is `8 9 6 7`.

**Row sums:** the outer loop runs over the rows, the sum is set to 0 **inside** it, the inner loop adds the elements of the row and the sum is displayed **after** the inner loop.

```java
int[][] sales = {
    {12, 7, 9, 5},
    {4, 15, 6, 9},
    {8, 3, 11, 2}
};
for (int i = 0; i < sales.length; i++) {
    int rs = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j < sales[i].length; j++)
        rs = rs + sales[i][j];
    System.out.println("Row " + i + ": " + rs);
}
```

Output: `Row 0: 33 / Row 1: 34 / Row 2: 24`.

**Column sums:** the loops change places: the outer loop over the columns (limit `a[0].length`), the inner loop over the rows (limit `a.length`).

```java
int[][] sales = {
    {12, 7, 9, 5},
    {4, 15, 6, 9},
    {8, 3, 11, 2}
};
for (int j = 0; j < sales[0].length; j++) {
    int cs = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < sales.length; i++)
        cs = cs + sales[i][j];
    System.out.println("Column " + j + ": " + cs);
}
```

Output: `Column 0: 24 / Column 1: 25 / Column 2: 26 / Column 3: 16`.

The sum of all the row sums equals the sum of all the column sums; both equal the sum of all the elements. Two arrays, one for rows and one for columns, give both kinds of totals in one pass.

In a **square matrix** (n rows and n columns) the **left (principal) diagonal** has the elements with `i == j`; the **right diagonal** has `i + j == n - 1`, that is `a[i][n - 1 - i]`.

```java
int[][] a = {
    {5, 2, 8, 1},
    {9, 4, 3, 7},
    {6, 5, 2, 3},
    {4, 8, 1, 6}
};
int ls = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
    ls = ls + a[i][i];
System.out.println(ls);
```

Output for the matrix of the slides: `17`; the right diagonal gives `13`.

For **odd n** the centre element `a[n / 2][n / 2]` is on both diagonals, so it is counted twice if the two sums are added; for **even n** no element is common. A condition `i == j || i + j == n - 1` adds each element once. A diagonal loop over a table that is not square can cause an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

